there are a lot of people out there who  want to talk trash about your family and  say that you're a child abuse victim  whatever and I'm here to say that's not  true i am nick gillespie with reason TV  and today we're talking with zach Wahls  the author of my two moms lessons of  love strength and what makes a family  Zach thanks for talking to us thanks  Riley you broke through into kind of  YouTube consciousness in 2011 tell us  about what were you testifying for and  what were you saying so I was testifying  for the Iowa House Judiciary Committee  during a public forum on hjr 6 which was  a constitutional amendment that would  have reversed the 2009 Supreme Court  decision that legalized same-sex  marriage in Iowa and the most immediate  after effect of that hearing on my life  was kind of this rocketing well you you  became a kind of poster boy for you know  the upside of same-sex marriage that you  know these families are not particularly  different from heterosexual marriages  you know what should people take away  from your story there were really two  key demographics I was trying to reach  with this book the first was other kids  who have same-sex parents they're just  kind of be a voice and say look I think  there are a lot of people out there who  want to talk trash about your family and  say that you're a child abuse victim  whatever and I'm here to say that's not  true and second also wanted to make sure  that I had a good message for people who  are on the fence and who thought you  know gay people they can do what they  want to do that's their right but in  terms how does that affect kids what do  you think is the biggest misconception  that outsiders have about children in a  same-sex marriage they're probably too  really large misconception the first is  that there's something inherently  immoral about homosexuality and  therefore the children will be somehow  immoral and as part of that maybe I  could corollary this you know this  thought that sexual sexual orientation  is a choice or a learned behavior in the  devil's own horrible and so that any  lesbians first off are always going to  have girls and they're going to turn  them into lesbian Thomas sexuals always  going to have boy kids and turn them gay  yeah yeah  in there i mean it's ridiculous reverse  anybody who actually knows you know a  same-sex couple it looked there are some  gay parents who have gay kids it's a  very small minority well in the book you  mentioned also that you know up at least  through this point in history  heterosexual parents are the leading  cause of gay kid that's right that's  true I mean nobody looks at Ellen  DeGeneres and says yeah those straight  parents are at it again yeah we're  talking not long after President Obama  has come out and said that he's in favor  personally anyway of same-sex marriage  what do you think of that is that a  positive development or is it a cynical  political ploy yeah you know I think  that you can have both be true I think  there's certainly a level of political  calculus that went into it but as  somebody who growing up has never been  represented by a president who believed  that the sanctity of my mother's unions  was valid to now have a sitting  president who supports them I don't  really care why he said it I care more  that he did in fact say it the gay  interests are usually allied with the  Democratic Party or with a liberal  economic intervention yet a lot of gay  people actually resent the state or  top-down control and regulation that  often comes with that how do your  politics play out particularly as the  son of a Velez being fo sho I  self-identify as an anti-authoritarian  liberal awesome kind of joke colloquy if  I'm a liberal tarian so to speak I think  there are certainly some instances where  you have corrosive state behavior and I  think gay people especially are very  attuned to this and even as the son of  the same sex couple who's not gay I was  very attuned to that they're also a  number of times though where state  intervention is completely necessary  schools are a great example you have a  lot of I've got a very close friend the  man who grew up in Grinnell Iowa about  10,000 people Grinnell College is in in  Grinnell Iowa and she went through some  really you know hard stuff growing up in  public school system because the way  that her teachers just didn't care and  more willing to step in on her behalf  when she was being mocked for having  same-sex parents there's a little bit  less true for me growing up in Iowa City  which is a more progressive community  thing Grinnell but at the end of the day  they're certainly going to be some  instances not necessarily many but  certainly some were stated intervention  in my opinion is warranted they were  going to be many more obviously with  like DOMA is a classic example there's a  defense of marriage and a sense of an  egregious violation of the ten  the moment to be sure but then also  there are some lots of other issues at  play there you run through a bunch of  the kind of characteristics of scouts  one that is not mentioned as a chapter  heading i believe is be prepared you  know if you're talking to America as a  country what should America be prepared  to understand going forward about issues  revolving around same-sex marriage and  about the families that come up in that  in that situation I think the the  biggest preparation so to speak is going  to have to be willing to make the effort  that it takes to understand somebody  coming from a different perspective  coming from that alternative family  alternative in terms of like a numbers  game not necessarily at normalcy game  that's a very kind of careful Walt line  to walk but in terms of people having to  sit down and really move past some of  these stereotypes and misconceptions  that we've seen be disproved not just  through empirical science but also  anecdotally with stories like mine and  you know some of the other two million  kids out there with an LGBT parent or  parents and so it's it's hard you know  and but that's ultimately the  responsibility that comes with the power  to shape public policy in this country  is you know the responsibility to sit  down and really work through these  issues on a person-to-person level and I  think it's going to take some  preparation but I think in a lot of ways  we're already moving towards that place  already right so I mean a positive  pluralism and if not necessarily an  approval of things in acceptance  tolerance but do your parents talk about  what it was like 20 30 or 40 years ago  versus now and I think everybody would  say there's you know there's been a lot  of progress but is it enough or when  when do we when will we stop writing  books about yeah coming you know where  the hook is oh I'm the kid of a zoo of a  game I hope yes really you know this is  certainly not something that I hope to  do for an extended amount of my life  because I hope it's something that  doesn't need to be done you know 10 15  years from now but they answered the  first part of the question you know  there was kind of this interesting kind  of you curve of what it was like having  lesbian parents and so far as how it was  seen by my peers when I was in like  kindergarten through third grade it was  like you have two moms hey I want to  moms like it was a very positive thing  and then fourth grade fifth grade it's  like you have you've gay parents that's  weird and then a lot of time I got the  high schools like dude you  lesbian parents are they hot yeah it's  like oh come on guys but I think more  broadly we are going to see that you  like flattened out you know my mom's  often kind of say like they never  thought they'd see same-sex marriage  legalized in their lifetimes let alone  in Iowa so there have definitely been  some pretty incredible developments even  in the last decade you've gone from  thirty-eight percent depending on the  pole two fifty three percent you've seen  really some seismic moves in that  direction that doesn't surprise me at  all we've been talking with zach Wahls  the author of the new book my two moms  lessons of love strength and what makes  a family for reason TV i'm nick  gillespie thanks Zach thanks  you

